Issues.Body:
## Preamble

This is related to #15477. After trying to find a fix, I realized that there are a few concerns with how controls are resized on DPI change.

Say we have a control with a panel placed on the bottom-right control of its parent. It is placed such that there is zero padding between the panel and the controls

<img width="386" height="293" alt="Form dimensions provided below" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/abc83afe-0375-45de-85d6-e8f12b0fc522" />

```
Form Size: {Width=400, Height=300}
Border Padding: {Left=8,Top=31,Right=8,Bottom=8}
Client Size: {Width=384, Height=261}
Panel Bounds: {X=184,Y=161,Width=200,Height=100}
```

The Border Padding is calculated by

```cs
[DllImport("user32.dll")]
private static extern int GetSystemMetricsForDpi(int nIndex, uint dpi);
private const int SM_CXSIZEFRAME = 32;
private const int SM_CYSIZEFRAME = 33;
private const int SM_CXPADDEDBORDER = 92;
private const int SM_CYCAPTION = 4;
private const int SM_CYMENU = 15;
public static Padding SizeFramePaddingForDpi(int dpi)
{
    int frameX = GetSystemMetricsForDpi(SM_CXSIZEFRAME, (uint)dpi);
    int frameY = GetSystemMetricsForDpi(SM_CYSIZEFRAME, (uint)dpi);
    int padding = GetSystemMetricsForDpi(SM_CXPADDEDBORDER, (uint)dpi);
    int caption = GetSystemMetricsForDpi(SM_CYCAPTION, (uint)dpi);
    return new Padding(
        frameX + padding,
        frameY + caption + padding,
        frameX + padding,
        frameY + padding);
}
```

If the Form undergoes a DPI change to 200%, we get the following

<img width="776" height="588" alt="Form dimensions provided below" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/09a66ee5-c49a-4a6f-b1db-043a81151936" />

```
Form Size: {Width=800, Height=600}
Border Padding: {Left=13,Top=58,Right=13,Bottom=13}
Client Size: {Width=774, Height=529}
Panel Bounds: {X=368,Y=322,Width=400,Height=200}
```

Note how there are now 6 pixels of padding on the right edge between the panel and form, and 7 pixels on the bottom edge.

The form size and the panel bounds perfectly double in size. However, according to the system metrics, the form's border doesn't not scale 1:1 with DPI. 

The preferred client rectangle gets calculated using [AdjustWindowRectExForDpi](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-adjustwindowrectexfordpi). It seems that this returns a pure scaling of the window bounds, not considering border padding. You can start tracing this fact [here](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/main/src/System.Windows.Forms/System/Windows/Forms/Form.cs#L4586)

## My Question

When doing a DPI scale, should we expect the child controls to respect their relative positioning within the client area? If so, then we are currently violating this.

If we were to comply with this idea, then the code changes would be rather significant, as a lot of the DPI size calculations rely on a scaling of the bounds, not the client area. I'm not saying it's impossible, however I do not know the ramifications of choosing this path, and hence am opening this issue in hopes of discussing the best course of action.
